-- Q1: medium / L2 — ACID Atomicity property
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Granite Bank processes a wire transfer that debits the sender's account and credits the recipient's account. Midway through processing, the database server loses power. When service resumes, the debit has been applied but the credit has not. Which ACID property was violated by this outcome?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Atomicity", "Consistency", "Isolation", "Durability"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5, SI-10. Atomicity requires that all operations within a transaction either complete entirely or are rolled back as a unit. A partial transaction — debit without credit — violates the all-or-nothing guarantee that protects data integrity. Wrong (B): Consistency ensures the database transitions between valid states but does not address partial transaction completion. Wrong (C): Isolation prevents concurrent transactions from interfering with each other, not partial execution of a single transaction. Wrong (D): Durability ensures committed transactions survive system failures, but this transaction was never fully committed.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Differential backup restore advantage
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Silverpoint Manufacturing runs a full backup every Sunday and differential backups Monday through Saturday. On Thursday morning, the file server fails. The IT manager must restore the server as quickly as possible. Which restore sequence should the IT manager follow?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Restore Sunday full, then Monday differential, then Tuesday differential, then Wednesday differential", "Restore Sunday full, then Wednesday differential only", "Restore Wednesday differential only without the full backup", "Restore Sunday full, then each daily incremental from Monday through Wednesday"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1, Section 5.2. A differential backup captures all changes since the last full backup. Restoring requires only the most recent full backup plus the latest differential — two restore operations total, regardless of how many differentials were taken. Wrong (A): Applying each differential sequentially is the procedure for incremental backups, not differentials. Wrong (C): A differential cannot be restored without the base full backup because it only contains changes. Wrong (D): This describes restoring from incremental backups, which requires the full plus every incremental in sequence.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Graph database use case
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Pinnacle Insurance is building a system to detect organized fraud rings by mapping relationships between claimants, medical providers, and repair shops. The development team needs a database that efficiently traverses chains of connections. Which NoSQL database type is BEST suited for this requirement?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Document store", "Key-value store", "Column-family store", "Graph database"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): COBIT 2019, DSS05.03. A graph database stores data as nodes and edges representing relationships, making it ideal for traversing complex networks such as fraud detection rings. It efficiently queries multi-hop relationship paths that would require expensive joins in relational databases. Wrong (A): Document stores manage JSON-like documents with flexible schemas but are not optimized for traversing relationship chains. Wrong (B): Key-value stores provide fast lookups by key but cannot efficiently traverse relationship paths between entities. Wrong (D): Column-family stores are optimized for large-scale analytics and time-series data, not relationship traversal.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Incremental backup definition
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$What does an incremental backup capture?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["All data in the database regardless of changes", "All data changed since the last full backup", "Only data changed since the most recent backup of any type", "Only data that has been flagged for archival"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1, Section 5.2. An incremental backup captures only the data that has changed since the last backup — whether that last backup was a full or another incremental. This minimizes backup time and storage but requires restoring the full chain for recovery. Wrong (A): Backing up all data regardless of changes describes a full backup, not an incremental. Wrong (B): Capturing all changes since the last full backup describes a differential backup. Wrong (C): This restates the incremental definition but is not the correct answer choice here.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Data user role
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$What is the primary responsibility of a data user under a data governance framework?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Follow established data usage policies when accessing data", "Approve data classification levels and access permissions", "Implement technical storage and security controls for databases", "Define retention schedules and destruction procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): COBIT 2019, APO01.06. A data user is anyone who accesses data to perform their job function. Their primary responsibility is to follow established usage policies, including access restrictions, handling requirements, and reporting obligations. Wrong (B): Approving classification levels and access permissions is the responsibility of the data owner, not the data user. Wrong (C): Implementing technical storage and security controls is the role of the data custodian. Wrong (D): Defining retention schedules is a governance function performed by data owners and the governance council.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Encryption in transit as sharing-phase control
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Harborview Credit Union transmits daily loan portfolio files to a third-party analytics vendor over the internet. The files contain customer Social Security numbers and account balances. The information security officer must implement a control appropriate for the sharing phase of the data lifecycle. Which control BEST addresses this risk?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Implement role-based access controls on the loan database", "Encrypt the files in transit using TLS and require the vendor to sign a data sharing agreement", "Store the loan portfolio files on encrypted drives at rest", "Run data quality validation checks before generating the files"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5, SC-8. The sharing phase requires controls that protect data during transmission to external parties. TLS encryption in transit prevents interception, and a data sharing agreement establishes the vendor's obligations for handling sensitive data. Wrong (A): Role-based access controls are a use-phase control, not a sharing-phase control. Wrong (C): Encryption at rest is a storage-phase control that does not protect data during transmission. Wrong (D): Data quality validation is a creation-phase control and does not address the risk of data exposure during sharing.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Column-family database use case
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Meridian Energy monitors 50,000 smart meters that each report power consumption readings every 15 minutes. The engineering team needs a database capable of writing millions of time-stamped records per hour and running aggregate queries across months of data. Which NoSQL database type is MOST appropriate?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Document store", "Key-value store", "Column-family store", "Graph database"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): COBIT 2019, DSS05.03. Column-family databases store data in columns rather than rows, making them highly efficient for write-heavy time-series workloads and aggregate analytics across large datasets. They support horizontal scaling to handle millions of writes per hour. Wrong (A): Document stores use flexible JSON-like schemas suited for content management, not high-throughput time-series ingestion. Wrong (B): Key-value stores provide fast lookups by key but lack the columnar structure needed for efficient time-series aggregation. Wrong (D): Graph databases are optimized for traversing relationships between entities, not for high-volume time-series data.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L2 — Encryption at rest as storage-phase control
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Westfield Accounting stores client tax returns on a cloud file server. The firm wants to protect this data while it resides in storage. Which control is MOST appropriate for the storage phase of the data lifecycle?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Data loss prevention rules on outbound email", "Input validation on data entry forms", "Role-based access logging during data processing", "Encryption at rest with access controls limited to authorized staff"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5, SC-28. Encryption at rest protects stored data from unauthorized access if physical or logical security is compromised. Combined with access controls, this is the primary storage-phase safeguard for confidential data. Wrong (A): Data loss prevention on outbound email is a sharing-phase control, not a storage-phase control. Wrong (B): Input validation is a creation-phase control applied when data is first entered. Wrong (C): Role-based access logging is a use-phase control that monitors data processing activities.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q9: easy / L2 — Input validation as creation-phase control
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$An accounts payable clerk at Ridgeline Services enters a vendor invoice into the ERP system. The system rejects the entry because the invoice date is in the future. Which data lifecycle phase does this control protect?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Creation", "Storage", "Use", "Sharing"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5, SI-10. Input validation rules that check data format, range, and reasonableness operate during the creation phase when data is first captured or entered. Rejecting a future-dated invoice prevents invalid data from entering the system. Wrong (B): Storage-phase controls address encryption at rest and physical security, not data entry validation. Wrong (C): Use-phase controls govern how data is accessed and processed after it has been stored. Wrong (D): Sharing-phase controls protect data during transmission to other parties.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Legal hold definition
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$What is a legal hold in the context of data retention?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A policy that requires data to be encrypted before storage", "A directive suspending the normal data destruction schedule for data relevant to pending litigation", "A backup strategy that preserves all data indefinitely", "A classification level applied to data subject to regulatory audit"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): AICPA AT-C Section 205.35. A legal hold (litigation hold) is a directive that suspends normal destruction schedules for data that may be relevant to pending or anticipated litigation, audit, or government investigation. The hold remains until formally released by legal counsel. Wrong (A): Encryption requirements are data security controls, not legal holds. Wrong (C): Preserving all data indefinitely describes an absence of retention policy, not a legal hold. Wrong (D): A legal hold is not a data classification level; it is a temporary suspension of destruction.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — SOX seven-year retention
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Beacon Consulting is establishing retention policies for its audit workpapers. The compliance officer references federal requirements for publicly traded company auditors. Under the Sarbanes-Oxley Act, what is the minimum retention period for audit workpapers?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["3 years from the audit date", "5 years from the audit date", "7 years from the date of creation", "10 years from the fiscal year-end"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): SOX Section 802 (18 U.S.C. § 1520). The Sarbanes-Oxley Act requires that audit workpapers and related records be retained for a minimum of seven years from the date of creation. Destruction before this period can result in criminal penalties. Wrong (A): Three years is the minimum IRS retention period for some tax records, not SOX audit workpapers. Wrong (B): Five years does not correspond to any standard SOX retention requirement. Wrong (D): Ten years exceeds the SOX minimum; no federal statute requires this specific period for audit workpapers.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Choosing backup strategy for fast restore
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Oakdale Regional Hospital's disaster recovery plan requires that its electronic health records system be restored within two hours of a failure. The current strategy uses weekly full backups with daily incremental backups, and a full restore cycle currently takes four hours. Which change to the backup strategy would MOST effectively reduce restore time?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Increase the frequency of full backups to twice weekly", "Switch to monthly full backups with daily incremental backups", "Perform full backups less frequently to reduce server load", "Replace daily incremental backups with daily differential backups"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1, Section 5.2. Switching from incremental to differential backups reduces restore time because only the last full backup plus the most recent differential are needed — two operations instead of restoring the full plus every daily incremental in sequence. Wrong (A): More frequent full backups reduce the number of incrementals but do not fundamentally change the sequential restore requirement. Wrong (B): Monthly full backups would increase restore time by requiring more incremental restores. Wrong (C): Less frequent full backups would worsen restore time, not improve it.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Data validity dimension
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Brighton Retail's point-of-sale system accepts a product code entry of "XYZ-999" for a transaction, but the company's product code format requires three digits followed by a hyphen and four letters. Which data quality dimension is violated?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Validity", "Accuracy", "Timeliness", "Completeness"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): COBIT 2019, APO14.04. Validity measures whether data conforms to defined formats and business rules. A product code that does not match the required format violates the validity dimension because it fails the structural rules governing that field. Wrong (B): Accuracy measures whether data correctly represents the real-world entity, not whether it matches a required format. Wrong (C): Timeliness measures whether data is available when needed and reflects current conditions. Wrong (D): Completeness measures whether all required data fields are populated, not whether values match a format.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Evaluating backup strategy trade-offs for compliance
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Whitmore Financial Services processes 200,000 transactions daily and must meet a regulatory requirement for 4-hour recovery point objective (RPO) and 2-hour recovery time objective (RTO). The current strategy uses nightly full backups. An auditor identifies that a midday failure could result in up to 18 hours of data loss. The IT director proposes three alternatives: (1) full backups every 4 hours, (2) nightly full with hourly incremental, or (3) nightly full with 4-hour differential. Which alternative BEST meets both the RPO and RTO requirements?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Full backups every 4 hours to satisfy the RPO", "Nightly full backup with hourly incremental backups", "Nightly full backup with 4-hour differential backups", "Maintain the current nightly full backup with no changes"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1, Sections 3.4 and 5.2. Hourly incrementals limit maximum data loss to one hour (well within the 4-hour RPO) and require restoring only the nightly full plus a small number of incrementals, achievable within the 2-hour RTO for 200K daily transactions. Wrong (A): Full backups every 4 hours meet the RPO but consume excessive resources and may cause performance degradation that threatens the RTO during high-volume processing. Wrong (C): Four-hour differentials meet the RPO but grow progressively larger throughout the day, potentially exceeding the 2-hour RTO for restoration. Wrong (D): Nightly full backups alone create an 18-hour data loss window that violates the 4-hour RPO requirement.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: easy / L2 — Key-value store use case
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Eastwood E-Commerce uses a NoSQL database that stores user session tokens as simple pairs — a unique session identifier mapped to session data — for rapid retrieval during checkout. Which NoSQL database type does this describe?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Document store", "Graph database", "Key-value store", "Column-family store"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): COBIT 2019, DSS05.03. A key-value store uses simple key-value pairs for extremely fast lookups. Session management is a classic use case because each session ID maps directly to its associated data, requiring only single-key retrieval. Wrong (A): Document stores manage flexible JSON-like documents, which is more complex than the simple key-value mapping described. Wrong (B): Graph databases model relationships between entities using nodes and edges, not simple key-value pairs. Wrong (D): Column-family stores organize data in columns for analytics and time-series workloads, not session token lookups.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Spoliation penalties for legal hold failure
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Cornerstone Manufacturing receives a litigation hold notice from legal counsel related to a product liability lawsuit. Despite the notice, the IT department follows its standard retention policy and deletes three years of product testing records during a scheduled purge. What is the MOST likely legal consequence?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The company receives a tax penalty for early record destruction", "The court issues a warning but allows the case to proceed normally", "The company's insurance carrier denies coverage for the litigation", "The court imposes spoliation sanctions, potentially including adverse inference against the company"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Federal Rules of Civil Procedure, Rule 37(e). Destroying data subject to a legal hold constitutes spoliation of evidence. Courts may impose sanctions including adverse inference instructions (jurors may assume the destroyed data was unfavorable), monetary penalties, or even default judgment in severe cases. Wrong (A): Tax penalties relate to IRS filing requirements, not destruction of litigation-relevant records. Wrong (B): Spoliation is treated seriously by courts and typically results in sanctions, not mere warnings. Wrong (C): Insurance coverage decisions are contractual matters unrelated to evidence preservation obligations.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Evaluating data governance maturity across roles
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Ridgecrest Healthcare's internal audit reveals the following findings: (1) the CISO unilaterally grants database access without consulting department heads, (2) data quality issues in patient records are reported to IT helpdesk tickets with no tracking, (3) no formal data classification policy exists, and (4) three departments maintain separate spreadsheets of the same patient demographic data. Which finding represents the MOST fundamental governance deficiency that, if remediated first, would create the foundation for addressing the other three?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The absence of a formal data classification policy", "The CISO granting access without consulting data owners", "The lack of data quality tracking and stewardship processes", "The redundant patient data across departments"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): COBIT 2019, APO01.06 and APO14.01. Data classification is the foundational governance control that drives all other decisions. Without classification, there is no basis for determining who should approve access (fixing finding 1), what quality standards apply (fixing finding 2), or how data should be consolidated (fixing finding 4). Wrong (B): The access control problem stems from the absence of classification — the CISO has no classification policy to guide access decisions. Wrong (C): Data quality tracking requires defined stewardship roles, which depend on classification to determine quality standards. Wrong (D): Redundant data is a symptom of missing governance; consolidation requires classification to determine the authoritative source.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Certificate of destruction purpose
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$What is the purpose of a certificate of destruction in data management?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["To authorize the creation of new database backups", "To document that data was permanently deleted in compliance with retention policies", "To classify data sensitivity levels before storage", "To grant users permission to access archived records"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): NIST SP 800-88 Rev. 1, Section 4.7. A certificate of destruction provides formal documentation that data was permanently destroyed using approved methods. It serves as evidence of compliance with retention policies and regulatory requirements, recording the date, method, and responsible party. Wrong (A): Certificates of destruction relate to data deletion, not backup creation. Wrong (C): Data classification is performed during the creation phase, not during destruction. Wrong (D): Access permissions are granted through access control mechanisms, not certificates of destruction.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Differential backup definition
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$What does a differential backup capture?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Only data that has never been backed up before", "Only data changed since the most recent backup of any type", "All data changed since the last full backup", "An exact copy of the entire database every time"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1, Section 5.2. A differential backup captures all data that has changed since the last full backup, regardless of any intervening differential or incremental backups. Each differential grows larger as more changes accumulate since the last full. Wrong (A): Differential backups include all changes since the last full, not just previously unbacked data. Wrong (B): Capturing only changes since the most recent backup of any type describes an incremental backup. Wrong (D): Copying the entire database every time describes a full backup.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Matching lifecycle controls to correct phase
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Sunridge Financial implements the following controls for its customer database: (1) TLS encryption on API endpoints that send data to a credit bureau, (2) AES-256 encryption on the database storage volumes, (3) format validation rules on the account opening form, and (4) role-based access with audit logging for analysts querying the database. Which control is correctly matched to the use phase of the data lifecycle?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["TLS encryption on API endpoints sending data to the credit bureau", "AES-256 encryption on database storage volumes", "Format validation rules on the account opening form", "Role-based access with audit logging for analysts"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5, AC-3 and AU-2. The use phase governs how data is accessed, processed, and analyzed. Role-based access controls with audit logging ensure that only authorized users query the database and all access is recorded. Wrong (A): TLS encryption on outbound API endpoints is a sharing-phase control that protects data during transmission. Wrong (B): AES-256 encryption on storage volumes is a storage-phase control protecting data at rest. Wrong (C): Format validation on account opening forms is a creation-phase control that ensures data quality at entry.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Segregation of duties in data governance roles
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$At Parkview Municipal Authority, the database administrator (data custodian) has been approving access requests for the financial reporting database because the CFO (data owner) is frequently unavailable. An IS auditor identifies this arrangement during a controls assessment. Why does this practice create a governance risk?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It violates the separation between business accountability and technical implementation, allowing access decisions without ownership oversight", "It increases storage costs because the database administrator may provision too many user accounts", "It slows database performance because each access approval requires a system restart", "It creates a backup vulnerability because access approvals are not included in incremental backups"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): COBIT 2019, APO01.06 and DSS05.04. Data governance requires separation between the data owner (who makes access decisions based on business need) and the data custodian (who implements technical controls). When the custodian approves access, business accountability is bypassed, and access may be granted without proper authorization. Wrong (B): Provisioning user accounts has negligible impact on storage costs; the risk is unauthorized access, not cost. Wrong (C): Access approvals do not require system restarts and have no impact on database performance. Wrong (D): Access approval records are administrative controls unrelated to backup procedures.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Full backup characteristics
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Which backup strategy requires the MOST storage space but provides the fastest restore time?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Incremental backup", "Full backup", "Differential backup", "Transaction log backup"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1, Section 5.2. A full backup copies all data every time, requiring the most storage space. However, restoration requires only a single operation — restoring that one backup — making it the fastest to recover from. Wrong (A): Incremental backups use the least storage but require restoring the full backup plus every incremental in sequence, making restoration the slowest. Wrong (C): Differential backups use moderate storage and require restoring the full backup plus the latest differential — faster than incremental but slower than a single full restore. Wrong (D): Transaction log backups capture database transaction logs, not complete data copies, and require the base plus all logs to restore.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Applying data classification to determine controls
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Northgate Insurance discovers that its claims processing system stores policyholder health records alongside general correspondence in the same database table with identical access controls. An IS auditor recommends remediation. Which action BEST aligns with data classification principles?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Delete all general correspondence to simplify access controls", "Apply the lowest classification level to all records for consistency", "Classify health records as restricted and general correspondence as internal, then apply controls matching each classification level", "Encrypt the entire table and grant all claims staff full access"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): NIST SP 800-60 Vol. 1, Section 2.2. Data classification assigns sensitivity levels that determine minimum security controls. Health records (HIPAA-regulated) warrant restricted classification with strong controls, while general correspondence requires only internal-level access. Segregating by classification prevents over- or under-protecting data. Wrong (A): Deleting correspondence addresses symptoms, not the classification gap, and may violate retention requirements. Wrong (B): Applying the lowest classification to all records would under-protect health records, violating HIPAA requirements. Wrong (D): Encrypting everything with full staff access provides encryption but fails to enforce need-to-know restrictions required for restricted data.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Archival integrity verification
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Crestview Mutual Fund archived seven years of trade confirmation records to offline tape storage. During a regulatory examination, the compliance team discovers that 15% of archived tapes produce read errors when loaded. Which archival control should have been implemented to prevent this data loss?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Encrypting the tapes with AES-256 before archival", "Limiting tape access to only the compliance department", "Classifying the trade confirmations as internal-use data", "Performing periodic integrity checks and media refresh on archived tapes"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5, MP-6 and SI-7. Archived data requires periodic integrity checks to detect media degradation before data loss occurs. Media refresh — copying data to new media before the old media's expected lifespan expires — preserves long-term accessibility. Wrong (A): Encryption protects confidentiality but does not prevent physical media degradation or read errors. Wrong (B): Restricting access controls who can read the tapes but does not address media deterioration. Wrong (C): Data classification determines security controls but does not directly prevent physical media failure.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Denormalized schema in data warehouse
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Bluewater Analytics is designing its financial data warehouse and debates whether to use a normalized or denormalized schema. The primary use case is complex reporting queries that aggregate data across multiple dimensions. Which schema design is MOST appropriate for this data warehouse, and why?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Denormalized, because it reduces the number of joins required for analytical queries and improves read performance", "Normalized, because it eliminates all data redundancy and ensures real-time transactional integrity", "Denormalized, because it enforces referential integrity more strictly than normalized schemas", "Normalized, because it optimizes aggregate query speed by storing pre-computed summaries"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): COBIT 2019, BAI03.01. Data warehouses use denormalized schemas (such as star or snowflake) to optimize read performance for analytical queries. Denormalization reduces the number of table joins needed, improving query speed at the cost of some data redundancy. Wrong (B): Normalized schemas minimize redundancy and suit transactional systems but require many joins that slow analytical queries. Wrong (C): Denormalized schemas actually relax some referential integrity constraints to gain performance — they do not enforce it more strictly. Wrong (D): Normalized schemas do not store pre-computed summaries; they eliminate redundancy, which requires more joins for aggregation.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: hard / L4 — Evaluating data destruction methods for SSD vs HDD
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Crossfield Bank is decommissioning 200 servers — 120 with traditional hard disk drives (HDDs) containing customer financial data and 80 with solid-state drives (SSDs) containing the same classification of data. The IT security team proposes using degaussing for all 200 servers to destroy the data before disposal. A compliance auditor reviews the proposal. What should the auditor conclude?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The proposal is acceptable because degaussing effectively destroys data on all storage media types", "The proposal is inadequate for the SSDs because degaussing does not affect flash memory, and the auditor should recommend cryptographic erasure or physical destruction for those drives", "The proposal is inadequate for the HDDs because degaussing only works on solid-state media", "The proposal should be replaced entirely with reformatting, which is sufficient for both media types"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): NIST SP 800-88 Rev. 1, Section 4.6. Degaussing uses magnetic fields to destroy data on magnetic media (HDDs, tapes) but has no effect on SSDs, which store data in flash memory chips. SSDs require cryptographic erasure (rendering encrypted data unrecoverable by destroying the keys) or physical destruction (shredding). Wrong (A): Degaussing is effective only on magnetic media; applying it to SSDs leaves data fully recoverable. Wrong (C): Degaussing works specifically on magnetic media like HDDs, not solid-state media. Wrong (D): Reformatting does not securely destroy data on either media type; data recovery tools can retrieve reformatted data.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q27: hard / L3 — HIPAA retention and destruction timing
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Lakewood Medical Center maintains electronic health records and associated documentation. The records management team identifies patient consent forms from 2018 that are no longer referenced by any active patient case. The forms were last effective in 2019. A staff member proposes destroying these records immediately to reduce storage costs. The compliance officer must determine whether destruction is permissible under HIPAA retention requirements. What should the compliance officer conclude?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Destruction is permissible because the forms are no longer referenced by active cases", "Destruction is permissible because HIPAA requires only three years of retention", "The forms must be retained until at least 2025 because HIPAA requires six years from the last effective date", "The forms must be retained indefinitely because HIPAA has no destruction provision"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): HIPAA 45 CFR § 164.530(j). HIPAA requires covered entities to retain documentation for six years from the date of creation or last effective date, whichever is later. With a last effective date of 2019, the earliest permissible destruction date is 2025. Destroying in 2024 or earlier would violate this requirement. Wrong (A): The absence of active case references does not override regulatory retention periods. Wrong (B): HIPAA requires six years, not three; the three-year period applies to certain IRS records. Wrong (D): HIPAA does not require indefinite retention; records may be destroyed after the six-year retention period expires.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q28: hard / L4 — Evaluating ETL reconciliation controls
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Apex Financial's ETL pipeline loads general ledger data from three source ERP systems into a central data warehouse nightly. The IT team implemented the following controls: (1) row count comparison between source and warehouse, (2) hash totals on key financial columns, (3) automated email alerts when variances exceed 0.1%, and (4) manual review of transformation exception logs weekly. An IS auditor evaluates these controls for SOX compliance. Which control gap should the auditor identify as the HIGHEST risk?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Row count comparison does not detect data modifications that preserve record counts", "Hash totals on financial columns may produce false positives during currency conversion", "Automated alerts at 0.1% threshold may miss immaterial variances", "Weekly manual review of transformation exceptions allows up to seven days of undetected transformation errors"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): COBIT 2019, DSS06.02 and SOX Section 404. Weekly review of transformation exceptions creates a window of up to seven days during which erroneous transformations could corrupt financial data undetected. For SOX compliance, transformation errors in financial data must be identified and corrected promptly. The other controls operate nightly, but the exception review lag undermines their effectiveness. Wrong (A): While row counts alone are insufficient, the hash totals (control 2) compensate by detecting modifications that preserve record counts. Wrong (B): Hash total false positives during currency conversion would trigger investigation, which is a conservative error, not a high-risk gap. Wrong (C): The 0.1% threshold is reasonable for financial data; immaterial variances below this level pose minimal reporting risk.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — DLP controls for data sharing
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Clearbrook Advisory discovers that an employee emailed a spreadsheet containing 500 client Social Security numbers to a personal Gmail account. The firm has encryption at rest and role-based access controls but no outbound data monitoring. Which control would MOST effectively prevent this type of data exfiltration?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Data loss prevention (DLP) rules that scan outbound communications for sensitive data patterns and block unauthorized transmissions", "Increasing the frequency of full backups to preserve data copies", "Implementing stronger password requirements for email accounts", "Adding encryption at rest to the email server's storage volumes"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5, SC-7 and PE-19. Data loss prevention (DLP) tools monitor outbound communications for sensitive data patterns such as Social Security numbers and can block or quarantine unauthorized transmissions. This is the primary sharing-phase control for preventing data exfiltration. Wrong (B): Backup frequency preserves data but does not prevent unauthorized transmission of sensitive information. Wrong (C): Stronger passwords protect against unauthorized account access but do not prevent an authorized user from emailing sensitive data. Wrong (D): Encryption at rest on the email server protects stored data but does not monitor or prevent outbound data exfiltration.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Evaluating cryptographic erasure vs physical destruction
(5, 'Data Management and Lifecycle', $EXPL$Ironwood Capital must decommission 500 encrypted SSDs from its trading floor servers. The data is classified as restricted. The CISO proposes cryptographic erasure — destroying the encryption keys to render the data unrecoverable — as a faster and more cost-effective alternative to physical destruction. The chief risk officer questions whether cryptographic erasure provides sufficient assurance. Under what condition is the CISO's proposal adequate for restricted data?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Only if the SSDs are also reformatted before cryptographic erasure", "Only if the encryption was implemented at the hardware level using validated algorithms and the key management system can verifiably confirm complete key destruction", "Only if the SSDs will be reused internally rather than disposed of externally", "Cryptographic erasure is never adequate for restricted data regardless of implementation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): NIST SP 800-88 Rev. 1, Section 4.5 and Appendix A. Cryptographic erasure is acceptable for restricted data when the encryption uses validated algorithms (e.g., AES-256 implemented in hardware), the encryption was active for the entire data lifecycle, and the key management system provides verifiable proof of complete key destruction. Without these conditions, residual risk remains. Wrong (A): Reformatting before cryptographic erasure adds no security value; the encryption renders data unreadable regardless of filesystem state. Wrong (C): The intended reuse or disposal of the drives does not change the adequacy of the destruction method; restricted data requires the same assurance regardless. Wrong (D): NIST SP 800-88 explicitly recognizes cryptographic erasure as an acceptable purge method when properly implemented with validated encryption.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;
